# SELinux policy patch applied by `SELINUX=late lab/vm.sh <name> start` (magiskpolicy --live),
# right before `setenforce 1`. One magiskpolicy statement per line; a line that does not apply to an
# image (unknown type) is skipped and recorded in run/<name>/shim.txt. "sh <cmd>" lines run <cmd> as
# root on the device.
#
# Why: none of the lab's live-CD images can run the whole platform enforcing in QEMU.
#   - Android-x86 7.1 / 9.0 with androidboot.selinux=enforcing boot-loop (init reboots at once);
#     enforcing late, zygote/ueventd/servicemanager are denied on the tmpfs-labelled live root and
#     the framework restarts.
#   - BlissOS 16 with androidboot.selinux=enforcing never finishes booting; a plain late
#     `setenforce 1` kills SurfaceFlinger (virgl ioctls / llvmpipe execmem) and rescue-party
#     reboots the VM.
#
# So the lab enforces what Lindroids is about — the APP SANDBOX — and leaves the platform
# permissive: every domain is made permissive, then the untrusted_app* domains (Termux, Lindroids,
# and anything else the user installs) are made enforcing again. Every access whose subject is
# Termux or Lindroids is then decided, enforced, by the image's own policy: app <-> app unix sockets
# (MLS categories), TCP sockets, app_data_file, executing Termux binaries, ioctls...
permissive *
enforce untrusted_app
enforce untrusted_app_25
enforce untrusted_app_27
enforce untrusted_app_29
enforce untrusted_app_30
enforce untrusted_app_32
#
# Emulator-only gaps of the app domains themselves (a real device labels these properly). Without
# them every app's RenderThread aborts, Termux included, before Lindroids is even reached:
# Mesa enumerates /dev/dri through sysfs; renderD128 is `device` on Android-x86 and gpu_device on
# Bliss; Bliss runs its FFmpeg Codec2 HAL in the init domain (Termux plays its bell through it).
allow appdomain sysfs file { read open getattr }
allow appdomain sysfs dir { read open search getattr }
allow appdomain graphics_device dir { read open search getattr }
allow appdomain gpu_device dir { read open search getattr }
allow appdomain gpu_device chr_file { read write open ioctl getattr map }
allowxperm appdomain gpu_device chr_file ioctl 0x0000-0xffff
allow appdomain device chr_file { read write open ioctl getattr map }
allowxperm appdomain device chr_file ioctl 0x0000-0xffff
# ...and MLS: apps (s0:cN,cM) may only write s0 objects that are mlstrustedobject. Adding `device`
# to that attribute live does not reach compiled constraints, so relabel the DRM nodes as
# gpu_device (what a real device uses). Lines starting with "sh " run as root on the device.
sh for f in /dev/dri/*; do chcon u:object_r:gpu_device:s0 $f; done
allow appdomain init binder { call transfer }
allow appdomain init fd use
